  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nIt was a quiet Monday afternoon across the region and that\nrelative quiet should continue into tonight. The only exception\nmay be a brief shower or storm over northeast Nebraska after\nmidnight. A decaying area of storms to our northwest may persist\ninto our area, but an increasingly stable airmass ahead of it\nshould limit its duration. Other than this, expect some clouds\nas temperatures cool into the middle 60s by morning. \n\nTuesday sees a shortwave trough move across the northern Plains.\nThis will result in the development of storms along a weak front\nover central Nebraska. These storms should reach our western\nborder by mid-afternoon before spreading southeast with time\ninto Tuesday evening. Rain chances have decreased since the \nprevious forecast as the latest model guidance keeps the bulk of\nthe storms over central Nebraska and moves them into north-\ncentral Kansas. In this scenario, more limited rain chances can \nbe anticipated, alongside a more conditional severe weather \nthreat. That said, the strongest storms may still produce large \nhail and damaging winds. Temperatures reach the middle 80s\nduring the afternoon. Thunderstorm chances gradually lessen \nTuesday evening as the trough exits the region and instability \nwanes.\n\nWednesday and Thursday end up being two pleasant days to look\nforward to. A cold front behind Tuesday's system drops\ntemperatures into the lower 80s with quiet weather both days. \nMostly sunny skies further adds to the enjoyable change. This \nchange is short-lived as a warming trend takes over on Friday. \nTemperatures climb through the 80s towards the lower 90s by this\nweekend as the southwestern US ridge builds towards the \nnortheast. It remains humid with passing weather disturbances to\nthe north of the region. This should support the return of at \nleast a low chance of rain, but with increased temperatures \ncomes greater near-surface stability to overcome. Hopefully, at\nleast some rain brings periodic relief from the heat.",
